The field names in JSON/postman body  → must match Java field names.
Field types must be compatible (e.g., Long should not be passed as a string).
If they don't match, you’ll get a 400 Bad Request or Spring won't populate the object.

By default, Spring Boot uses Jackson, and it maps JSON → Java by matching JSON keys to Java field names.
e.g. = private String categoryName;  {  "categoryName": "Electronics"     }

If you want the JSON field to use a different name than your Java field, use:
@JsonProperty("custom_name")    |{  "custom_name": "Electronics"  } //pass this in postman
private String categoryName;    |

If your Java field is declared as Long (or long) and you pass a String in the JSON for that field,
Spring (via Jackson) will try to convert it.

| Java Type           | JSON Value Type          | Example JSON Input             | Behavior                        |
| ------------------- | ------------------------ | ------------------------------ | ------------------------------- |
| `int` / `Integer`   | Number or numeric String | `"42"` or `42`                 | ✅ Converted to `int`            |
| `long` / `Long`     | Number or numeric String | `"10000"` or `10000`           | ✅ Converted to `long`           |
| `double` / `Double` | Number or numeric String | `"12.34"` or `12.34`           | ✅ Converted to `double`         |
| `float` / `Float`   | Number or numeric String | `"3.14"` or `3.14`             | ✅ Converted to `float`          |
| `BigDecimal`        | Number or numeric String | `"123456.789"` or `123456.789` | ✅ Converted to `BigDecimal`     |
| `int` / `long` etc. | Non-numeric String       | `"abc"`, `"ten"`               | ❌ Will throw a conversion error |

